  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 235 PM EDT Tue Jul 28 2026\n\nAn upper-level low continues to slowly track east through Ontario \nand into Quebec as of midday, dragging lingering clouds along with \nit. A secondary area of stratus is noted across central Michigan, \nattempting to seep south while stratus persists down wind of Lake \nMichigan amid northeasterly flow. A touch breezy in spots this \nafternoon with gusts near 20 mph thanks to the departing low. These \ngusts have produced 4 to 5 foot waves on Lake Michigan where \ndangerous swimming conditions are expected through Wednesday morning.\n\nWith high pressure becoming established tonight, lows will be in the \nupper-50s humid conditions will continue to fade. High pressure \nlingers through most of this week while the upper-air pattern \ncontinues to be characterized by a towering 500mb ridge over the \nFour Corners. We remain on the northeast side of this ridge keeping \nthings relatively seasonable. However, by the end of the week, \nvarious shortwaves enter the upper-level flow presenting the chance \nfor showers and thunderstorms. There is growing agreement that low \npressure develops over the Northern Plains and arrives to Indiana \nFriday night through Saturday morning, representing our next best \nchance for rain.",
